# How the derivative works — the engine shows its work
#: The Pasos{} operator differentiates SHOWING each rule —generated by the engine, not written by hand—: the sum rule distributes the derivative term by term, and the power rule lowers the exponent with its arithmetic (c·ξⁿ → c·n·ξⁿ⁻¹). With tic/toc we measure how long the engine takes to do all that work.

## 1 · The shape function (linear)
#: Here L/2 is constant, so the derivative is simple; even so you can see the sum rule (each term separately) and that the constant gives 0:
tic
dl = Pasos{(1+xi)/2 * L @ xi}
toc

## 2 · A polynomial with powers — here the power rule DOES operate
#: With real powers the arithmetic is visible: each term c·ξⁿ lowers its exponent and multiplies it, leaving c·n·ξⁿ⁻¹. In 3·ξ² the 2 comes down (3·2·ξ¹ = 6ξ); in 2·ξ it leaves 2·1 = 2:
tic
dp = Pasos{3*xi^2 + 2*xi @ xi}
toc

## 3 · A cubic
#: The exponent 3 comes down and multiplies the coefficient, and the power is left at 2:
tic
dc = Pasos{xi^3 @ xi}
toc
